1
00:00:00,643 --> 00:00:04,078
Let's take ComPort Zone through a real
workflow.

2
00:00:04,298 --> 00:00:10,579
We'll connect to a simulated instrument, send
commands, automate a test, and watch a

3
00:00:10,579 --> 00:00:11,701
live dashboard.

4
00:00:11,921 --> 00:00:17,659
The tutorial device runs locally, so you can
follow along without any hardware.

5
00:00:19,335 --> 00:00:22,300
Start with Connection, then Connection
Settings.

6
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:25,592
Each terminal tab has its own connection.

7
00:00:25,812 --> 00:00:30,954
For this walkthrough, the demonstration server
is already running on this computer.

8
00:00:31,174 --> 00:00:33,819
The manual explains how to start it.

9
00:00:35,479 --> 00:00:37,186
Choose TCP.

10
00:00:37,406 --> 00:00:41,737
Enter the local address shown here, and port
fifty twenty five.

11
00:00:41,957 --> 00:00:44,687
Select carriage return plus line feed.

12
00:00:44,907 --> 00:00:49,643
For your own instrument, use its address and
required line ending.

13
00:00:51,288 --> 00:00:53,336
Accept the settings to connect.

14
00:00:53,556 --> 00:00:56,329
Watch the connection indicator turn green.

15
00:00:56,549 --> 00:01:01,008
Before sending anything, check the target
shown in the terminal header.

16
00:01:01,228 --> 00:01:04,684
This keeps commands going to the device you
intended.

17
00:01:06,335 --> 00:01:10,623
Click the transmit prompt and type the
identification query.

18
00:01:10,843 --> 00:01:11,995
Press Enter.

19
00:01:12,215 --> 00:01:16,844
The outgoing command appears, followed by the
device's reply.

20
00:01:17,064 --> 00:01:21,872
Here, the model and serial number confirm that
we're talking to the tutorial

21
00:01:21,872 --> 00:01:22,568
instrument.

22
00:01:24,216 --> 00:01:27,374
Now ask for voltage, current, and status.

23
00:01:27,594 --> 00:01:31,007
Each reply appears in the terminal as it
arrives.

24
00:01:31,227 --> 00:01:36,390
The simulated instrument returns changing
measurements and a ready state.

25
00:01:36,610 --> 00:01:40,492
You can inspect the conversation while
continuing to type.

26
00:01:42,135 --> 00:01:45,761
To repeat a command, press the Up arrow at the
prompt.

27
00:01:45,981 --> 00:01:49,587
The previous command comes back into the
editable line.

28
00:01:49,807 --> 00:01:51,812
Press Enter to send it again.

29
00:01:52,032 --> 00:01:55,616
This is useful when you're checking a changing
value.

30
00:01:57,289 --> 00:02:03,198
Start typing a familiar command prefix and
press Control Space to open completion

31
00:02:03,198 --> 00:02:04,073
suggestions.

32
00:02:04,293 --> 00:02:08,709
You can choose a suggestion or dismiss the
popup with Escape.

33
00:02:08,929 --> 00:02:11,873
Let's clear the prompt before the next
example.

34
00:02:13,545 --> 00:02:18,217
Press Control F and enter part of a command to
search the terminal.

35
00:02:18,437 --> 00:02:21,829
Matching text is highlighted in the
conversation.

36
00:02:22,049 --> 00:02:25,889
Escape closes the search and returns you to
your work.

37
00:02:27,544 --> 00:02:30,104
Switch the input mode to Hex Bytes.

38
00:02:30,324 --> 00:02:35,018
These byte pairs spell ping followed by
carriage return and line feed.

39
00:02:35,238 --> 00:02:37,286
Send them and look for PONG.

40
00:02:37,506 --> 00:02:40,514
Then switch back to Text for normal commands.

41
00:02:42,172 --> 00:02:45,030
A write command may produce no reply.

42
00:02:45,250 --> 00:02:49,581
Set the voltage to fifteen, then query the
voltage separately.

43
00:02:49,801 --> 00:02:52,468
The returned value confirms the change.

44
00:02:52,688 --> 00:02:58,085
Finally, reset the demonstration instrument so
the remaining examples start from a

45
00:02:58,085 --> 00:02:58,874
known state.

46
00:03:00,527 --> 00:03:05,178
Let's turn the identification query into a
reusable action.

47
00:03:05,398 --> 00:03:08,576
Open Tools and choose Add Saved Command.

48
00:03:08,796 --> 00:03:13,831
This saves typing and makes a common operation
available from the drawer.

49
00:03:15,497 --> 00:03:21,503
Give the command a short label, enter the
identification query, and put it in a

50
00:03:21,503 --> 00:03:22,643
Tutorial group.

51
00:03:22,863 --> 00:03:24,634
Confirm the dialog.

52
00:03:24,854 --> 00:03:30,145
The label helps you recognize the action
without reading the entire command.

53
00:03:31,826 --> 00:03:34,194
Open Saved Commands in the drawer.

54
00:03:34,414 --> 00:03:37,187
Click the send action beside Identify.

55
00:03:37,407 --> 00:03:43,599
The actual query runs in the selected
terminal, and its reply appears just as it did

56
00:03:43,599 --> 00:03:44,852
when we typed it.

57
00:03:46,515 --> 00:03:49,182
Click the star beside the saved command.

58
00:03:49,402 --> 00:03:51,215
Now open Favorites.

59
00:03:51,435 --> 00:03:55,744
The same action is available here, ready for
repeated checks.

60
00:03:55,964 --> 00:04:00,764
Use favorites for a small set of commands you
reach for most often.

61
00:04:02,430 --> 00:04:08,654
To share these commands, open File, Import and
Export, then choose Export Saved

62
00:04:08,654 --> 00:04:09,363
Commands.

63
00:04:09,583 --> 00:04:13,615
Save the CSV file in a location you can find.

64
00:04:13,835 --> 00:04:18,294
Your teammate can import that library into
their own installation.

65
00:04:19,939 --> 00:04:22,072
Next, let's automate a check.

66
00:04:22,292 --> 00:04:28,223
Use File, Open Command File Editor, and select
the tutorial's first check file.

67
00:04:28,443 --> 00:04:33,520
This opens an editable script in a workspace
tab beside the terminal.

68
00:04:35,188 --> 00:04:40,997
The script identifies the device, repeats a
voltage measurement, checks the value,

69
00:04:40,997 --> 00:04:42,484
and prints a summary.

70
00:04:42,704 --> 00:04:45,862
Add a comment and press Control S to save.

71
00:04:46,082 --> 00:04:49,836
We're editing a working copy of the supplied
example.

72
00:04:51,503 --> 00:04:53,999
Check the run target above the editor.

73
00:04:54,219 --> 00:04:57,291
It should be the connected tutorial terminal.

74
00:04:57,511 --> 00:04:58,684
Click Run.

75
00:04:58,904 --> 00:05:04,834
Because this script contains a parameter,
ComPort Zone asks for its value before

76
00:05:04,834 --> 00:05:06,094
execution starts.

77
00:05:07,759 --> 00:05:10,746
Set the repeat count to five and click Start.

78
00:05:10,966 --> 00:05:14,592
Switch to the terminal to watch the commands
execute.

79
00:05:14,812 --> 00:05:16,711
Click Pause during the run.

80
00:05:16,931 --> 00:05:22,584
The controls now offer Resume, so you can
inspect progress before continuing.

81
00:05:24,261 --> 00:05:28,101
Click Resume and watch the remaining
measurements arrive.

82
00:05:28,321 --> 00:05:31,927
The script handles the repeated commands and
checks for us.

83
00:05:32,147 --> 00:05:37,459
Wait for the run to finish before starting
another operation on this connection.

84
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:42,214
The final log reports five passing checks.

85
00:05:42,434 --> 00:05:46,572
Read the terminal output as well as the
completion status.

86
00:05:46,792 --> 00:05:51,639
Together they show what was sent, what came
back, and whether the script's

87
00:05:51,639 --> 00:05:53,342
conditions were satisfied.

88
00:05:55,012 --> 00:05:58,596
Return to the editor and choose Split Right
from View.

89
00:05:58,816 --> 00:06:01,867
Drag the divider to give each pane enough
space.

90
00:06:02,087 --> 00:06:07,962
A split workspace is useful when you want the
command file and a terminal visible

91
00:06:07,962 --> 00:06:08,615
together.

92
00:06:10,269 --> 00:06:12,637
Open the command palette from Tools.

93
00:06:12,857 --> 00:06:15,353
Search for Join Tabs and press Enter.

94
00:06:15,573 --> 00:06:17,920
The workspace returns to one pane.

95
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:22,940
The palette gives you a quick route to actions
without navigating menus.

96
00:06:24,597 --> 00:06:27,797
Now let's build a live view of the same
device.

97
00:06:28,017 --> 00:06:32,775
Open Tools, then Control Panels, to manage
your saved panels.

98
00:06:32,995 --> 00:06:39,043
Panels collect measurements, status
indicators, and device controls in one
workspace

99
00:06:39,043 --> 00:06:39,331
tab.

100
00:06:40,980 --> 00:06:45,993
Click Import and select the tutorial control
panel JSON file.

101
00:06:46,213 --> 00:06:48,688
The imported panel appears in the list.

102
00:06:48,908 --> 00:06:54,941
The repository includes this example so you
can reproduce the dashboard without

103
00:06:54,941 --> 00:06:57,079
entering every tile by hand.

104
00:06:58,738 --> 00:07:01,639
Select the imported panel and click Open.

105
00:07:01,859 --> 00:07:05,422
Measurements begin updating from the connected
instrument.

106
00:07:05,642 --> 00:07:11,432
Notice the numeric readings, the ready
indicator, and the controls grouped together

107
00:07:11,432 --> 00:07:12,618
in the same view.

108
00:07:14,257 --> 00:07:18,609
Open the connection selector and choose the
tutorial device.

109
00:07:18,829 --> 00:07:23,181
The binding chip shows which terminal supplies
the panel's connection.

110
00:07:23,401 --> 00:07:28,308
Always check this before using a panel with
multiple devices open.

111
00:07:29,986 --> 00:07:32,908
Right click the voltage tile and choose Edit.

112
00:07:33,128 --> 00:07:39,550
A tile defines the command to send, how to
interpret the reply, and when to poll.

113
00:07:39,770 --> 00:07:44,100
You can adjust an existing tile as your
workflow changes.

114
00:07:45,776 --> 00:07:50,618
Change the polling interval to seven hundred
fifty milliseconds and save.

115
00:07:50,838 --> 00:07:53,398
Choose a rate your device can handle.

116
00:07:53,618 --> 00:07:59,496
Faster updates can be helpful, but every poll
still consumes time on the underlying

117
00:07:59,496 --> 00:08:00,274
connection.

118
00:08:01,933 --> 00:08:05,069
Watch the voltage and power readings update.

119
00:08:05,289 --> 00:08:11,043
The device simulator varies its measurements,
so these are fresh responses rather

120
00:08:11,043 --> 00:08:12,606
than a static display.

121
00:08:12,826 --> 00:08:15,215
The panel keeps polling while we watch.

122
00:08:16,891 --> 00:08:20,219
Double click the voltage tile to open its
chart.

123
00:08:20,439 --> 00:08:23,298
Change the visible time span to one minute.

124
00:08:23,518 --> 00:08:29,531
The trace makes it easier to spot movement
over time than reading individual numbers

125
00:08:29,531 --> 00:08:29,961
alone.

126
00:08:31,648 --> 00:08:36,469
Move the pointer across the chart to inspect
values along the trace.

127
00:08:36,689 --> 00:08:40,892
This helps you compare earlier readings with
the current measurement.

128
00:08:41,112 --> 00:08:43,906
Use Back to return to the tile dashboard.

129
00:08:45,568 --> 00:08:49,088
Let's deliberately enable a fault on the
simulator.

130
00:08:49,308 --> 00:08:54,470
Send the demonstration fault command in the
terminal, then return to the panel.

131
00:08:54,690 --> 00:09:00,549
The status indicator changes, showing how an
unexpected device state appears during

132
00:09:00,549 --> 00:09:01,325
monitoring.

133
00:09:02,997 --> 00:09:05,685
Click the bell to inspect the panel's alerts.

134
00:09:05,905 --> 00:09:11,324
Use this view to see what needs attention
while the dashboard continues updating.

135
00:09:11,544 --> 00:09:15,874
Close the alert view when you're ready to
investigate the device.

136
00:09:17,508 --> 00:09:21,369
Return to the terminal and clear the simulated
fault.

137
00:09:21,589 --> 00:09:25,173
Back on the panel, wait for the next status
response.

138
00:09:25,393 --> 00:09:31,025
The indicator returns to its normal state,
confirming the device has recovered.

139
00:09:32,694 --> 00:09:37,195
A panel can arm automatically when its bound
device connects.

140
00:09:37,415 --> 00:09:39,186
Check the lock control.

141
00:09:39,406 --> 00:09:43,459
Click to disarm, then click again to arm
before writing.

142
00:09:43,679 --> 00:09:48,607
This makes the difference between monitoring
and changing the instrument visible.

143
00:09:50,249 --> 00:09:54,452
Click the output control to switch the
simulated supply off.

144
00:09:54,672 --> 00:09:57,381
Watch the related measurements respond.

145
00:09:57,601 --> 00:10:02,649
You're sending a real command to the local
simulator through the panel's bound

146
00:10:02,649 --> 00:10:03,361
connection.

147
00:10:04,995 --> 00:10:07,278
Click again to restore the output.

148
00:10:07,498 --> 00:10:10,463
The measurements recover on subsequent polls.

149
00:10:10,683 --> 00:10:16,543
For your own hardware, understand the effect
of each write command before using its

150
00:10:16,543 --> 00:10:17,531
panel control.

151
00:10:19,187 --> 00:10:24,307
Enter fifteen in the voltage setpoint control
and press Enter to send.

152
00:10:24,527 --> 00:10:27,855
Watch the measured voltage move to the new
level.

153
00:10:28,075 --> 00:10:34,074
A separate readback is useful confirmation
that the device accepted the requested

154
00:10:34,074 --> 00:10:34,667
setting.

155
00:10:36,309 --> 00:10:40,000
Disarm the controls when you're finished
changing settings.

156
00:10:40,220 --> 00:10:44,721
The write buttons become unavailable while
monitoring continues.

157
00:10:44,941 --> 00:10:49,997
This is a useful habit when leaving a
dashboard open for observation.

158
00:10:51,654 --> 00:10:56,689
Click the panel's CSV logging control and
choose an output file.

159
00:10:56,909 --> 00:11:00,599
Logging records measurement events for later
analysis.

160
00:11:00,819 --> 00:11:06,183
Save the file somewhere separate from the
application files so you can find it

161
00:11:06,183 --> 00:11:06,665
easily.

162
00:11:08,321 --> 00:11:13,207
Let a few polls arrive, then click the logging
control again to stop.

163
00:11:13,427 --> 00:11:16,861
The CSV now contains recorded values.

164
00:11:17,081 --> 00:11:22,031
You can open it in a spreadsheet or use it in
your own analysis tools.

165
00:11:23,659 --> 00:11:27,349
The same device is available from the command
line.

166
00:11:27,569 --> 00:11:33,756
In this PowerShell console, run a single
identification query against localhost.

167
00:11:33,976 --> 00:11:40,461
The actual reply appears below the command,
and a zero exit code confirms success.

168
00:11:42,120 --> 00:11:45,363
Run validate on the tutorial command file.

169
00:11:45,583 --> 00:11:49,402
This checks the script without opening a
device connection.

170
00:11:49,622 --> 00:11:55,936
Use validation after editing a file and before
adding it to an automated workflow.

171
00:11:57,601 --> 00:12:03,190
Now run that file against the local device
with the repeat count set to three.

172
00:12:03,410 --> 00:12:07,528
Non interactive mode keeps parameter handling
explicit.

173
00:12:07,748 --> 00:12:11,972
Watch the measurements and the summary report
three passing checks.

174
00:12:13,635 --> 00:12:20,355
For a datagram example, send ping to the
simulator's UDP endpoint and expect PONG.

175
00:12:20,575 --> 00:12:24,159
The command checks the reply and exits
successfully.

176
00:12:24,379 --> 00:12:29,328
The tutorial server supports both transports
on the same local port.

177
00:12:30,996 --> 00:12:35,668
Return to the desktop app and reset the
demonstration instrument.

178
00:12:35,888 --> 00:12:39,387
The panel continues to reflect the live device
state.

179
00:12:39,607 --> 00:12:45,751
You've now used the terminal, reusable
commands, scripts, charts, controls, logging,

180
00:12:45,751 --> 00:12:47,287
and the command line.

181
00:12:48,940 --> 00:12:51,521
Disconnect the terminal when you're finished.

182
00:12:51,741 --> 00:12:57,320
To repeat this walkthrough, use the example
files and instructions linked with the

183
00:12:57,320 --> 00:12:57,864
project.

184
00:12:58,084 --> 00:13:03,238
The English manual provides the detailed
reference, and the release includes

185
00:13:03,238 --> 00:13:06,425
captions and chapter timestamps for this
video.
